    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday, we need to use the concept that the probability of independent events occurring is the product of their individual probabilities.\n\nHere's the step-by-step reasoning and calculation:\n\n1. The probability that it will rain on Saturday is \\(60\\%\\).\n2. The probability that it will rain on Sunday is \\(25\\%\\).\n\nSince the events are independent, we multiply the probabilities of each event occurring to find the probability that both events occur.\n\n\\[\n\\text{Probability of rain on both days} = P(\\text{Rain on Saturday}) \\times P(\\text{Rain on Sunday})\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Probability of rain on both days} = 60\\% \\times 25\\%\n\\]\n\nTo convert percentages into decimals for calculation:\n\\[\n60\\% = 0.6\n\\]\n\\[\n25\\% = 0.25\n\\]\n\nNow, we can find the product:\n\\[\n\\text{Probability of rain on both days} = 0.6 \\times 0.25\n\\]\n\nPerform the multiplication:\n\n\\[\n0.6 \\times 0.25 = 0.15\n\\]\n\nNext, we convert this result back into a percentage by multiplying by 100:\n\n\\[\n0.15 \\times 100 = 15\\%\n\\]\n\nSo, the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday is \\boxed{15\\%}.",
